A||||5||JohnnyK||Figured out hard starting/rough running problem||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||11-16-2000||04:26 PM||||While trying to start my 4cyl, I noticed a real, real strong gas smell.. I figured, it's probably because it's not firing.. A few minutes later, it gets pretty bad, so I take off the air cleaner housing, and the TBI is still dripping! (Pretty badly, too). Now, I assume it's not supposed to do that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> What someone told me, is since it is dripping, all my pressure is leaking out, and all the gas sits on top of the tbi plate. And the rest is obvious.. Sound right?||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||11-16-2000||06:02 PM||||Sounds right to me.  Back in the carburetor days, you knew the float needed adjusting, if you saw gas dripping into the carb throat when the car wasn't running.  Today, a dripping TB injector means it's leaking, I guess.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-16-2000||08:52 PM||||Get the rebuilt kit for the TBI. This is less expancive than the injector is. If the injector does end up being shot then you still want to go thru the rest of the TBI and replace all the seals and the fuel reg diaphram.<P>The kit has all the seals and gaskets and the fuel reg diaphram. It's time consuming but not hard to do.<P>Note, the injector dripping doesn't always mean a bad injector. The injector is sealed in the TBI with a couple O rings. One of those rings may have quit, letting fuel run down between the TBI body and the injector shell.<P>Also, unplug the injector to make sure it's not getting any power. If the ECM or wiring have a problem they could be holding the injector open.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||11-17-2000||01:35 PM||||I went to my local junkyard and grabbed a tbi off a car to see if that would help. Thing cost me $40 Canadian. Did I get ripped? ||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||11-17-2000||06:29 PM||||I see...! Thanks, Ogre - might be a  useful bit of knowledge, for my 1988 (4 cyl).  ||||reg||10
Z||000005||theogre||11-17-2000||06:41 PM||||john, $40can is like $30-35us I think. That's pretty cheap for a decent used TBI. Just the TPS or IAC cost that or more to buy a new one.<P><BR>Stu,<P>As you know, the 88 uses a dif TBI. If you find it leaking around the fuel pressure reg then don't go and buy just the reg diaphram. It's in the rebuild kit and the kit is only a few dollars more than just the regulator part.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-17-2000).]||||reg||1
